 SE: The 'output' property of the 'result' array is directly outputted into an HTML div without proper sanitation.
BL: `echo "<div class='calculation-result'>{$this->result['output']}</div>"`;
BUG FOUND: YES

 SE: The user input from the GET request is directly echoed into a JavaScript alert without sanitation, which can lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks.
BL: `alert('Hello, <?php echo $name; ?>!');`
BUG FOUND: YES
</solution>

The bug exists because the $name variable, which comes from user input, is directly used in the JavaScript alert without any sanitization. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts into the alert box by manipulating the 'name' parameter in the URL. To fix this issue, you should always sanitize any user input before using it in dynamic content generation. In this case, you can use the htmlspecialchars() function to sanitize the $name variable before using it in the JavaScript alert.

Corrected code:

<script>
$(document).ready(function() {
    $('h1').click(function() {
        alert('Hello, ' + <?php echo htmlspecialchars($name, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8'); ?> + '!');
    });
});
</script>

 SE: The '$_GET['name']' variable is directly echoed into JavaScript code without proper sanitation, which can lead to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS).
BL: `alert('Hello, <?php echo $name; ?>!');`
BUG FOUND: YES

Explanation:
The user input from the URL parameter 'name' is directly used in the JavaScript alert function. If an attacker provides malicious JavaScript code as input, it will be executed in the user's browser. To prevent XSS attacks, always sanitize user inputs and use functions that automatically escape output for you, such as the `htmlspecialchars()` function in PHP.

Corrected code:

```php
alert('Hello, <?php echo htmlspecialchars($name); ?>!');
```

or even better, avoid using user input in JavaScript code by setting the value in a DOM element instead:

```php
<body>
    <h1 id="welcome-message">Welcome, on our website</h1>
    <script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js"></script>
    <script>
        $(document).ready(function() {
            $('h1').click(function() {
                alert('Hello, <?php echo htmlspecialchars($name); ?>!'); 
            });
        });
    </script>
</body>
```

and then update the JavaScript code:

```php
alert('Hello, ' + $('#welcome-message').text().slice(10) + '!');
```
